3. **JavaScript Functionality**: The JavaScript you've started is on the right track. Ensure that clicking on the emotion toggles the display of the associated popup.

```javascript
function myFunction() {
    var popup = document.getElementById("happyPopup");
    popup.classList.toggle("show");
    // Hide any other popups if needed
}
```

4. **Handling Multiple Emotions**: If you're going to have multiple emotions, consider using a common class for the popups and adjusting the JavaScript to handle clicks dynamically.

```html
<div class="emotion-thumb" onclick="togglePopup('happyPopup')">
    <p>Happy
        <span class="popup-thumb-span" id="happyPopup" style="display:none;">I praise you, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made. Wonderful are your works; my soul knows it very well. Psalm 139:14</span>
    </p>
</div>
<div class="emotion-thumb" onclick="togglePopup('sadPopup')">
    <p>Sad
        <span class="popup-thumb-span" id="sadPopup" style="display:none;">Blessed are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted. Matthew 5:4</span>
    </p>
</div>
```

```javascript
function togglePopup(popupId) {
    const popups = document.querySelectorAll('.popup-thumb-span'); // get all popups
    popups.forEach(p => p.classList.remove('show')); // hide all popups
    const popup = document.getElementById(popupId);
